  "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-1) \\\\ z-0 & =1(x-1) \\\\ z & =x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ z=x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x-1$ (or $x-z+-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it.",
